--TEST--
`Core\Validate::isAscii` over a whole string is the conjunction of its answer over each of that string's code points, and is true exactly where the byte count equals the code-point count — both counted over one sweep, and both independent of where in the subject the offending character sits — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 12

--FILE--
<?nvs
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/validate.rs`'s module doc calls `isAscii` "a byte scan
// with no decoding at all", and `validate-ascii-and-printable-name-their-own-bounds.nvst`
// pins where that scan's line falls on a *single* code point (`DEL` accepted,
// `U+0080` refused). What no case asks is the property that makes it a scan
// rather than a look: over a whole string the answer must be the conjunction of
// the answers over its parts, at every length and at every position.
//
// Two independent statements of the same thing are asserted here by counting,
// so a member that read only the first character, only the first byte, or
// stopped at the first multi-byte sequence still answers plausibly on any one
// line and fails the totals.
final class Ascii {
    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        return $b ? "y" : "n";
    }

    // The conjunction, computed one code point at a time. `Core\Str::codePoints`
    // answers an `array<uint>` and `fromCodePoint` takes one, so a character is
    // round-tripped through the pair rather than sliced out by offset.
    public static function everyCharIsAscii(string $s): bool {
        bool $all = true;
        foreach (Core\Str::codePoints($s) as uint $c) {
            if (!Core\Validate::isAscii(Core\Str::fromCodePoint($c))) {
                $all = false;
            }
        }
        return $all;
    }

    // ASCII is the one encoding where UTF-8 spends exactly one byte per code
    // point, so this equality *is* `isAscii`, arrived at without asking it.
    public static function isOneBytePerChar(string $s): bool {
        return Core\Bytes::length($s as bytes) == Core\Str::length($s);
    }
}

// Sixteen subjects: empty, pure ASCII of three lengths, the two bounds as
// single characters, and ten mixtures that place a non-ASCII character first,
// last and in the middle of otherwise-ASCII text. The empty string is the first
// row for a reason — the module doc records it answering `true` where every
// `ctype_*` answers `false`, and under this case's reading that is not a
// special case at all but the conjunction's identity over no characters.
array<string> $rows = [
    "",
    "a",
    "abc",
    "0123456789 ~!",
    "\x7f",
    "\u{80}",
    "é",
    "aé",
    "éa",
    "aéb",
    "aaaaaaaaé",
    "éaaaaaaaa",
    "aaaaéaaaa",
    "日本",
    "a日b",
    "aaa\u{80}",
];

// Every subject answered three ways. The two counts are the assertion: all 16
// agree with the conjunction, and all 16 agree with the byte/character
// equality. Either count landing below 16 names a subject the scan did not
// reach the end of.
int $conjunction = 0;
int $byteCount = 0;
string $answers = "";
foreach ($rows as string $s) {
    bool $whole = Core\Validate::isAscii($s);
    if ($whole == Ascii::everyCharIsAscii($s)) {
        $conjunction = $conjunction + 1;
    }
    if ($whole == Ascii::isOneBytePerChar($s)) {
        $byteCount = $byteCount + 1;
    }
    $answers = $answers . Ascii::yn($whole);
}
echo "answers ", $answers, "\n";
echo "agree ", $conjunction as string, "|", $byteCount as string, "\n";

// Position stated once more on its own, because the sweep above proves the
// agreement rather than the direction: one `U+0080` anywhere in a run of eight
// ASCII characters is refused wherever it sits, and the run without it is
// accepted. A member scanning a fixed prefix passes exactly the last of these.
echo "position ",
     Ascii::yn(Core\Validate::isAscii("aaaaaaaa")),
     Ascii::yn(Core\Validate::isAscii("\u{80}aaaaaaa")),
     Ascii::yn(Core\Validate::isAscii("aaaa\u{80}aaa")),
     Ascii::yn(Core\Validate::isAscii("aaaaaaa\u{80}")),
     "\n";

// The same property from the other end: a subject grown one ASCII character at
// a time stays ASCII for as long as it is grown, and the moment a non-ASCII
// character is appended it never becomes ASCII again however much ASCII
// follows. Two runs of a hundred, counted.
int $stillAscii = 0;
string $grown = "";
int $i = 0;
for (; $i < 100; $i = $i + 1) {
    $grown = $grown . "a";
    if (Core\Validate::isAscii($grown)) {
        $stillAscii = $stillAscii + 1;
    }
}

int $stillRefused = 0;
string $tainted = "é";
int $j = 0;
for (; $j < 100; $j = $j + 1) {
    $tainted = $tainted . "a";
    if (!Core\Validate::isAscii($tainted)) {
        $stillRefused = $stillRefused + 1;
    }
}
echo "runs ", $stillAscii as string, "|", $stillRefused as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
answers yyyyynnnnnnnnnnn
agree 16|16
position ynnn
runs 100|100
